class:: FractionalNoise
summary:: Stationary finite-band approximation to 1/f power-law noise
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>Processes
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/FBrownianMotion, Classes/PinkNoise

description::

Fractional Noise produces a stochastic signal with a specific power spectral density, directly corresponding to colors of noise (like pink or brown) but allowing for continuous interpolation of the spectral slope. Mathematically, it serves as the audio-rate, time-derivative counterpart to fractional Brownian motion. Conceptually, it grants precise control over the texture and frequency bias of generated noise, making it perfect for synthesizing environmental sounds (wind, rain, rushing water) or specifically colored noise profiles.

Approximates power spectral density proportional to code::1/f.pow(alpha):: over a finite frequency band. It sums normalized, logarithmically spaced first-order Ornstein–Uhlenbeck components with independent unit-variance uniform innovations.

This is a stationary finite-band approximation, not an ideal infinite-memory fractional process. Like link::Classes/PinkNoise::, alpha 1 is pink-like; unlike PinkNoise, the slope, approximation band, quality, seed, and reset are explicit.

subsection:: Spectral view

image::fractional-noise-spectra.png#Idealized log-frequency/log-power references for alpha = 0, 1, and 2, shown respectively in muted teal, blue, and plum. FractionalNoise approximates these slopes only inside its finite minFreq-to-maxFreq band.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: alpha
Target power exponent, clipped to 0 through 2.
argument:: minFreq
Positive lower approximation frequency in hertz.
argument:: maxFreq
Upper frequency in hertz, clipped below the UGen Nyquist frequency.
argument:: quality
Init-rate OU component count, clipped to 4 through 32.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Clear component state and restore the seed on a rising edge.
returns:: Approximately unit-variance audio-rate noise.

method:: kr
Control-rate version with defaults suited to the control-rate Nyquist range.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
{ FractionalNoise.ar(1) * 0.1 }.play
)
::

code::
(
{ FractionalNoise.ar(MouseX.kr(0, 2)) * 0.1 }.play
)
::

code::
(
{ BPF.ar(FractionalNoise.ar(1.4), 1200, 0.2) * 0.2 }.play
)
::

code::
// Cinematic Howling Wind
(
{
    var gust, slope, sig, verb;
    // Intermittent gusts of wind
    gust = LFNoise2.kr(0.5).unipolar.pow(2); 
    // Slope modulates between deep rumbling (Brown, -2) and hissing (Pink, -1)
    slope = gust.linlin(0, 1, -2, -0.5);
    sig = FractionalNoise.ar(slope);
    // Resonant sweeping filters for the howling effect
    sig = BPF.ar(sig, gust.linexp(0, 1, 150, 1200), 0.2) * gust;
    verb = FreeVerb2.ar(sig, sig, 0.5, 0.9, 0.1);
    LeakDC.ar(verb * 4.0)
}.play;
)
::
